  "summary": "Four candidates directly combine a transformer-style self-attention encoder with electricity-consumption prediction: Haddad et al. report test MAE 0.93 on ProtMap [V0126]; Gupta et al. report accuracy 0.86 on GridWatch [V0125]; Brennan et al.'s deployment comparison reports F1 0.72 on ChurnBench [V0128]; Adebayo et al. report AUROC 0.65 on FireScope [V0127]. I rank [V0126] first because MAE is the most forecasting-aligned metric, followed by [V0125], [V0128], and [V0127] as the reported metrics become less demand-forecasting-aligned; scalar values are not comparable across MAE, accuracy, F1, and AUROC. Because exactly five IDs are required, [V0131] is appended only as the nearest electricity-consumption study, but it uses decentralised training without pooled records, with F1 0.76 on FireScope [V0131], not a transformer, so it should not be treated as evidence for transformer-based demand forecasting.",
